{
  "summary": "Addressed the five open hazards from round 4 with implementation-grounded fixes informed by the actual codebase structure: (1) extend the poison/cancellation mechanism to in-flight implementation tasks by adding a pre-phase dependency check in phases.py's run_implementation that aborts early if any depends_on ID has moved to 'rejected' since the task was queued, reusing the same context-dict poison flag pattern proposed for planning; (2) auto-clear the override field by adding a post-resolution hook in resolve_dependencies() that sets override=False on any feature whose unresolved_deps and dangling_deps are both empty, ensuring the override indicator disappears once blocking dependencies resolve without requiring manual cleanup; (3) resolve the LRU eviction deadlock when all 50 dependency_events are unaddressed by raising the cap to 100 only for unaddressed events (addressed events still capped at 50 with LRU), and adding a 'truncated_count' field to the event list metadata so the planning prompt can note that earlier events were lost; (4) add a fourth UI state 'dangling' (orange broken-link icon) distinct from blocked (red lock) and overridden (yellow warning) by extending the Go FeatureSummary with a DanglingDeps []string field and rendering it in the WebUI kanban card with a tooltip listing the missing IDs and a 'fix references' action button; (5) implement a token-budget strategy for dependency context in planning prompts by summarizing dependency status in a compact table format (one line per dependency: ID, title truncated to 40 chars, stage, resolution status) capped at 20 dependencies, and including only the 5 most recent unaddressed dependency_events with full detail while older ones get a one-line count summary. Additionally identified that the current FeatureFile serialization pattern (where _save() writes the entire _data dict) means depends_on and dependency_events conditional persistence requires explicit cleanup in _save() to omit empty lists, matching how done_script is already handled — a small but easy-to-miss implementation detail."
